Subcutaneous hematoma from blunt object strike to anterior shin
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Tense anterior shin swelling, Intact overlying skin, Blunt strike mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-026
Reason for Visit: Acute, rapidly expanding tense lump on the shin following a blunt strike, requiring urgent assessment for tibial fracture or compartment syndrome.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute blunt strike mechanism to the anterior tibia resulting in rapid, tense swelling and localized pain. Urgent assessment was required to definitively clear a tibial shaft fracture and rule out impending anterior compartment syndrome before the next available daytime appointment, avoiding irreversible ischemic muscle damage.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I walked directly into the heavy coffee table and my shin immediately grew a hard, purple egg.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a direct blow from a hard, blunt furniture edge occurring 2 hours ago. This resulted in a rapidly forming, tense, localized dome-shaped swelling overlying the anterior tibial shaft. Deep purple and blue discoloration developed quickly beneath the intact epidermis. Focal tenderness is strictly localized to the physical area of the hematoma swelling. The pain is primarily associated with direct pressure on the tense hematoma rather than a deep, persistent bony ache. The patient maintains the ability to fully weight-bear and ambulate on the affected leg.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently, avoiding direct touch to the anterior shin.
Social/Safety Concerns: Heavy furniture placement in high-traffic walking areas poses a recurrent fall/strike risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, visibly protecting the lower leg from accidental contact.
Physical Exam: Lower limb examination reveals tense anterior shin swelling. The overlying skin remains completely intact without breaks, lacerations, or extensive abrasions. Palpation confirms the swelling is a superficial fluid collection without deep, severe bony point tenderness on the tibial crest itself beneath the hematoma. Intact active ankle dorsiflexion and plantarflexion occur without exacerbation of the localized shin pain. Anterior compartment remains soft and compressible beyond the hematoma border. Normal distal pedal pulses and sensory examination are noted in the foot.
Environmental Observations: Heavy oak coffee table identified as the mechanism; advised spatial rearrangement.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. No anticoagulants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Subcutaneous hematoma to anterior shin - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Tibial shaft fracture - Ruled out due to: Preserved weight-bearing ability and lack of severe, deep bony point tenderness or mechanical instability.

Anterior compartment syndrome - Ruled out due to: Soft adjacent muscle compartments, normal distal sensation, and pain-free active ankle dorsiflexion.

Deep vein thrombosis - Ruled out due to: Isolated anterior traumatic mechanism, absence of posterior calf swelling, and lack of generalized leg edema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The anterior shin is highly vulnerable to traumatic hematomas due to the lack of subcutaneous adipose tissue over the tibia. Bedside clinical assessment definitively differentiated this superficial hematoma from a high-pressure anterior compartment syndrome or underlying tibial fracture by confirming intact distal neurovascular status and a pain-free range of ankle motion. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically necessary to exclude these limb-threatening emergencies, provide immediate reassurance, and implement acute localized compression therapy, bypassing an unnecessary emergency department visit for a plain film radiograph.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated strict RICE protocol (Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation). Recommended paracetamol for analgesia. Advised that formal needle aspiration is contraindicated due to the high risk of introducing bacterial infection into the sterile blood collection.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a firm tubular compression bandage and rest with the leg elevated above the level of the heart. The mechanical counter-pressure stops further vascular leaking into the tissue space, directly reducing the tense, throbbing pain caused by skin stretching.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Tubular compression bandage, reusable ice pack.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to monitor hematoma resolution and ensure no secondary skin breakdown occurs over the tense area.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if the foot suddenly becomes cold, pale, or numb, if you develop agonizing pain when pointing your toes upward, or if you become entirely unable to put weight on the leg.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Subcutaneous hematomas on the shin absorb very slowly due to poor local blood supply and constant gravity dependency.
Clinical Course: The lump will become firmer over the first week before slowly flattening out over 4 to 8 weeks. Gravity will likely cause the bruising to track downward toward the ankle and foot over the coming days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if the swelling rapidly expands around the entire lower leg, or if you lose feeling between your first and second toes.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The "egg" is a pocket of blood trapped between the skin and the shin bone. The bone itself is completely unharmed. The body will act like a sponge and slowly reabsorb this blood.
Actionable Steps: Wear the compression bandage during the day. Elevate the leg whenever seated. Do not attempt to pop or drain the lump with a needle.

